!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/32/ltr8/http/meta-http-1.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/32/m/meta-kernel.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/32/m/meta-kernel.tn"
@doc:"""
  A meta-schema adding HTTP operations to the type system.

  SKETCH. Resolves as written; the orders API beside it does not yet. See sketch/README.md.

  Governed directly by the meta-kernel, which is not a stylistic choice: declaring a type
  constructor (the ~ marker) is permitted only in a schema whose own !!meta is the kernel. So this
  is a SIBLING of meta.tn, not a layer above it.

  operation is ~top & rather than ~product & or ~sum & because no data value ever has an operation
  as its type -- the same reasoning meta-kernel's own reference and instance_template use, where
  product would oblige an entry to supply access_pattern and size_type as meaningless filler.

  The point of the whole exercise: request and responses are type_ref, not text. An operation names
  the types it carries, the resolver checks they exist, and identity travels through the import
  graph. A description that names a type nothing declares fails to load, rather than failing at a
  client months later.
  """
{
  http_method => !enum [GET POST PUT PATCH DELETE HEAD OPTIONS]

  @doc:"An HTTP status code. Refined rather than a bare integer, so 42 is not a status."
  status_code => !integer ^ { min: 100  max: 599 }

  parameter_location => !enum [PATH QUERY HEADER]

  @doc:"""
    A parameter carried outside the body.

    type is a type_ref like everything else here, but it must name a scalar: a URL segment or a
    header value cannot carry a record. Nothing in this vocabulary enforces that, which is an open
    question -- see sketch/README.md.
    """
  parameter => {
    name:     text
    in:       parameter_location
    type:     type_ref
    required: boolean
  }

  @doc:"body is absent for a response that carries none -- a 204, or a schema document served as bytes."
  response => {
    status: status_code
    body:   type_ref?
  }

  @doc:"""
    One operation. A list of responses rather than a map keyed by status, for two reasons: a
    response is likely to grow members beyond its body, and a map-typed slot cannot hold a template
    parameter (the structure-templates CR's template_argument has no collection case), so a map
    would foreclose templated operations before they are tried.
    """
  operation => ~top & {
    method:     http_method
    path:       text
    parameters: [parameter]
    request:    type_ref?
    responses:  [response]
  }
}
